<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1823 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Holly and the Ivy</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old English folk carol about the holly tree at Christmas. The tune we sing was written down from a woman's singing in 1909.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the present melody collected by Cecil Sharp from Mary Clayton in 1909)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the country people and townspeople of England who kept and sang folk carols</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England (the present version from Chipping Campden, Gloucestershire)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traced to the early 19th century; the present version collected 1909, published 1911</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; words on Birmingham broadsides of the early 19th century, the present words and tune published by Cecil Sharp in 1911)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-holly-and-the-ivy">The Holly and the Ivy</a>&quot; is a traditional British folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a>, number 514 in the Roud Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Index. The song itself can be traced only as far back as the early nineteenth century, but its words reflect a link between holly and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> that goes back at least to medieval times. Since then the plant has carried a Christian symbolism: in the carol the holly stands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the ivy for his mother, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. The sharp leaves have been read as a reminder of the crown of thorns and the red berries of drops of blood, and holly and ivy are recorded regularly as church decorations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advent">Advent</a> and Christmas in churchwardens' accounts of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries.</p><p>The words occur in three broadsides published in Birmingham in the early 19th century. An early mention of the title is in William Hone's Ancient Mysteries Described of 1823, which lists it among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carols</a> &quot;now annually printed&quot; that Hone owned. The complete words appear in a book review of 1849, whose anonymous writer took them from a collection of carols &quot;printed in the cheapest form, at Birmingham&quot;, and praised such religious ballads as common in country districts and the humbler quarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s great towns, many of them kept only by word of mouth. Sylvester's A Garland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Carols (1861) printed the words and said they came from &quot;an old broadside, printed a century and a half since&quot;, that is, around 1711; Husk's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of the Nativity (1864) remarked that the carol had nearly escaped the notice of collectors and was still printed on the Birmingham broadsides.</p><p>In traditional communities the words and the melody varied greatly. The early nineteenth-century sources give no music, and several late nineteenth-century collections set the words to an &quot;old French carol&quot; in D minor. The words were sung to many folk melodies; three further tunes were collected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a> alone, and recordings survive of Peter Jones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ross-on-wye">Ross-on-Wye</a>, Herefordshire, and of Bessie Wallace of Camborne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, recorded in the early 1930s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> Carpenter. The most popular traditional variant seems to have been &quot;The Holly Bears a Berry&quot;, and the &quot;Sans Day Carol&quot;, regarded as a variant of the song, has a different tune.</p><p>The version now sung everywhere was collected by the English folk song collector Cecil Sharp from Mary Clayton in the market town of Chipping Campden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>. Sharp's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of her singing of the third verse is dated 13 January 1909 and is kept in the Cecil Sharp Manuscript Collection at Clare College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. He published the words and the melody in English Folk-Carols in 1911, the book that fixed the carol in its present form. The melody keeps to the six notes of a hexachord.</p><p>Sir Henry Walford Davies wrote a choral arrangement that is often sung at the Festival of Nine Lessons and Carols and by choirs around the world. Sharp also linked the carol to an older one, which he described as &quot;The Contest of the Ivy and the Holly&quot;, a contest between the traditional emblems of woman and man.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A folk carol that praises the holly above all the trees of the wood and takes its parts one by one: blossom, berry, prickle and bark. Each is set beside Mary bearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and beside what he would suffer. A refrain of sunrise, running deer, organ music and choir singing returns after every verse. The mood is bright, with the Passion in the background.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly and the ivy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When they are both full grown,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of all the trees that are in the wood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly bears the crown.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The rising of the sun</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the running of the deer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The playing of the merry organ,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sweet singing in the choir.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly bears a blossom,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As white as the lily flower,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Mary bore sweet Jesus Christ,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To be our sweet Saviour.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly bears a berry,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As red as any blood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Mary bore sweet Jesus Christ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For to do us sinners good.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly bears a prickle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As sharp as any thorn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Mary bore sweet Jesus Christ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morn.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly bears a bark,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As bitter as any gall.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And Mary bore sweet Jesus Christ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For to redeem us all.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly and the ivy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When they are both full grown.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of all the trees that are in the wood.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The holly bears the crown.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional, as collected by Cecil Sharp, English Folk-Carols (1911). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/English_Folk-Carols%2FThe_Holly_and_the_Ivy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
